    "body": "GE Vernova announced on Nov. 6 that it has been officially chosen by PowerGrid Corporation of India Limited (PGCIL) to deliver a critical 6-GW High-Voltage Direct-Current (HVDC) transmission corridor. The project was booked in the fourth quarter of 2026.\nThis project will serve as a vital energy highway, enabling the efficient transport of 6 GW of power from the renewable energy-rich region of Barmer, Rajasthan, to the load center in South Kalamb.\nBy connecting remote renewable energy zones to the national network, this 800-kV project aims to provide the infrastructure needed to stabilize power flow for millions of homes and businesses. As India’s electricity demand continues to grow, this high-capacity link is essential to ensuring that the country’s grid remains reliable and resilient while supporting its decarbonization goals.\nKey Takeaways Project will facilitate the transmission of 6 GW of clean power from the renewable energy zone in Barmer, Rajasthan, to support national energy demand. The project was booked in the fourth quarter of 2026. Reinforces GE Vernova’s commitment to modernizing India’s power system, ensuring greater stability and efficiency for the national grid.\n“India is at the forefront of the global energy transition, characterized by an unprecedented scale of renewable integration and rapidly evolving grid requirements,” said Philippe Piron, president and CEO of GE Vernova’s Electrification segment. “This project represents more than just transmission capacity; it is an investment in the reliability and future-proofing of India’s power system. We are proud to work with PGCIL to deploy the advanced technology required to bridge the gap between renewable energy production and national consumption, ensuring long-term system performance.”\n“As India accelerates its renewable energy journey, the development of robust, high-capacity transmission infrastructure is paramount,” said Sandeep Zanzaria, CEO and managing director at GE Vernova, T&D India. “The Barmer–South Kalamb HVDC corridor is a strategic project for PGCIL, designed to efficiently integrate large-scale renewable capacity from Rajasthan into the national grid. By leveraging advanced transmission technologies, we are strengthening the backbone of India’s power system, ensuring reliable, high-quality electricity reaches consumers across the country while supporting our national commitment to a sustainable and resilient energy future.”\nGE Vernova will deliver the complete design for the ±800-kV, 6,000-MW (2 x 3000-MW) bipolar HVDC link between Barmer II and South Kalamb. The project scope encompasses the creation of new converter stations, the supply of all major equipment, and the full management of site erection, testing, and commissioning. By overseeing the end-to-end delivery of this link, GE Vernova ensures a synchronized approach that prioritizes grid stability and system efficiency, suitable for large-scale renewable energy links such as Barmer.\nThe project will feature GE Vernova’s most advanced LCC (line commutated converter) valve, which will be manufactured at the Pallavaram factory in India. It will also include the eLumina control system, which provides the digital intelligence required to manage complex power flows, enabling the grid to respond dynamically to changing conditions.\nThis collaboration further strengthens GE Vernova’s long-standing relationship with PGCIL, reinforcing a shared vision of a modernized, resilient, and decarbonized power grid for the future.\nAbout GE Vernova GE Vernova is a purpose-built global energy company that includes Power, Wind, and Electrification segments and is supported by its accelerator businesses. Building on over 130 years of experience tackling the world’s challenges, GE Vernova is uniquely positioned to help lead the energy transition by continuing to electrify the world while simultaneously working to decarbonize it. GE Vernova helps customers power economies and deliver electricity that is vital to health, safety, security, and improved quality of life. GE Vernova is headquartered in Cambridge, Massachusetts, U.S., with more than 75,000 employees across 100+ countries.\nTagged in:\nBarmer\neLumina\nGE Vernova\nHVDC\nIndia\nLCC Valve\nPallavaram\nPGCIL\nRajasthan\nSouth Kalamb",
